{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>I will terrify.</b> Like וְהָמַמְתִּי, and its Targum is וֶאֱשַׁגֵּשׁ. And so every word whose root has a doubled final letter — when it is turned to speak in the conjugation of 'I did,' there are places where it drops the doubled letter and gives a dagesh to the other letter and vowels it with a qamatz, such as וְהַמֹּתִי — from the root וְהָם; וְהָם גִּלְגַּל עֶגְלָתוֹ (Isaiah 28); וְסַבֹּתִי (Ecclesiastes 2) — from the root סָבַב בֵּית אֵל (I Samuel 7:16); דַּלֹּתִי (Psalms 116) — from the root דָּלְלוּ וְחָרְבוּ (Isaiah 19); עַל כַּפַּיִם חַקֹּתִיךָ (Isaiah 49:16) — from the root חִקְקֵי לֵב (Judges 5); אֶת מִי רַצֹּתִי (I Samuel 12:3) — from the root רִצַּץ עָזַב דַּלִּים (Job 20). And the one who translates וְהַמֹּתִי as 'and I will kill' — is mistaken, for if it were from the root מִיתָה, its heh would not have a patach, nor would its mem have a dagesh, nor would it be vowelized with a qamatz, but rather וְהֵמַתִּי — like וְהֵמַתָּה אֶת הָעָם הַזֶּה (Numbers 14:15) — and the tav has a dagesh because it comes in place of two tavs: one is the root letter — since there is no מִיתָה without a tav — and the other is the functional ending, like אָמַרְתִּי, חָטָאתִּי, עָשִׂיתִי. And so too וְנָתַתִּי — the tav has a dagesh, because it comes in place of two, since it would need three tavs: two for the root, like בְּיוֹם תֵּת יהוה (Joshua 10), מַתַּת אֱלֹהִים הִיא (Ecclesiastes 3), and the third for function." "<b>The neck.</b> That they will flee before you and turn their necks toward you."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:54:19.344078431Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}